{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.domain_types`\n\n`Domain::NodeData` is the application-defined type that identifies a graph node across runs. Two `NodeData` values compare equal exactly when they identify the same node.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Durable identity used to intern this node.\n    type NodeData: Clone + Eq + Hash + Send + Sync;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests cover each obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.NodeData`. The assessment is from the supplied test sources and search excerpts. I did not read any implementation source.\n\n1. **Trait bound `Clone + Eq + Hash + Send + Sync`.**\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]` contains the helper `check_bounds`. It instantiates `identity::<G::NodeData>()`, which requires exactly `Clone + Eq + Hash + Send + Sync`, on a generic `G: Domain`.\n   - That compile-time check fails if the declared bound is loosened. Its `Identity` fixture derives only `Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash`, so it fails if the trait demands extra bounds such as `Debug`, `Serde` or `Display`.\n\n2. **Equal exactly when the same node: equal data gives the same node.**\n   - `fn[clones_interning_the_same_data_concurrently_get_one_handle]` interns `Node(42)` from four cloned caches at once. It asserts that every handle is equal and that `node_data` returns `Node(42)`.\n   - `fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]` interns `Identity(1)` repeatedly. Every repeated intern is accepted as the same node: it gives the same `node_path` and the expected successor handles.\n\n3. **Equal exactly when the same node: different data gives a different node.**\n   - `fn[clones_interning_the_same_data_concurrently_get_one_handle]` asserts `intern_node(Node(43)) != ids[0]`.\n   - `fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]` asserts that `node_path` differs for `Identity(1)` and `Identity(2)`.\n\n4. **Identifies a node across runs.** `fn[saved_node_and_edge_inputs_survive_a_different_handle_allocation_order]` saves evidence from one application. It then reloads it in a fresh application with a different handle allocation order. The data-based identity is resolved to the new handles. The saved inputs are still current, and they become stale when the underlying edges change.\n\nThe cited `requirements.sdk.graph.domain_types` is context only. All three tests carry a `{test}` reference to the subject, so there is no marking gap. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
